From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Mr. Wilson's assertions

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   > From: drw@bourbaki.mit.edu
   > 
   > The problem is that the choices available to one are not independent
   > of the choices made by another.  If enough people prefer to shop at
   > Wal-Mart, the other stores cease to exist, and I no longer have the
   > choice to pay the small premium of shopping at the traditional stores.
   > Etc.  The amount of economic benefit one has to sacrifice in order to
   > obtain security depends on the choices that other people make; thus,
   > people who crave security often have a strong interest in making
   > other people make the same choice.

   Okay, that does it. I'm sick of this thread. You are now claiming not that 
   taking a boring job provides economic safety but that buying in small 
   stores provides THE SHOPPER, NOT THE SHOPKEEPER, with economic safety.
   I see no reason whatsoever to explain such a view. Indeed, I suspect you
   cannot come up with such a link.

I'm not sure exactly what you mean, here.  I said "security", that is,
lack of change in one's economic circumstances.  To replace a
traditional downtown with a Wal-Mart is a change, and tends to cause
some people to have to change jobs, etc.  The introduction of
mass-produced bread often causes fresh-baked bread to become harder to
obtain, or more expensive, because the number of people willing to pay
the premium for fresh-baked bread is not adequate to support a bakery
(in many rural areas), etc.

Philosophically, you look upon yourself as an "economic mercenary" (I
think that was the word you used), as a rational agent attempting to
maximize your "utility" in some way.  Most people don't.  They'd like
to improve their utility in some way, but what they really want is to
obtain a job that pays them "enough", and then arrange that they can
never, ever be fired or laid off.  Giant bureaucracies, be they
governmental or commercial, have always attracted workers.  It's a
stultifying life, but it's secure.  And they loathe the economic
mercenaries.  "Greed" has been among the seven deadly sins for, what,
2000 years?

As for proof, I can only point to the last 60 or so years of elected
governments in the US, which have generally proclaimed that it as
their goal to improve "economic security" and have sacrificed economic
opportunity in order to do so.  I've not noticed any large movement
among the voters to change this.  (Although, of course, it's what the
Libertarian Party is trying to do.)  Hell, the "radical" movements
that attract a significant number of voters (or at least, press) have
tended to be "populist" movements that are even more intensively into
increasing "economic security", "economic justice", or "fairness".

As far as I can tell, most people really *don't* want to fight for
their maximum economic benefit.  They want to get "enough" (that is,
about as much as their neighbors), and they don't want to work very
hard to get it, and they want some authority to make sure that some
economic mercenary with strong motivation and a taste for hard work
doesn't come along and disrupt the system.

It's not a pretty picture.

Dale

