From: Duncan Frissell <76630.3577@CompuServe.COM>
Subject: This Too Shall Pass...

Vis a vis the questions from the Brothers Goebel and others on government:

On Gradualism - 

William Lloyd Garrison was faced with the same question vis a vis slavery.
He said something on the order of: "Urge immediate Abolition.  It will, 
alas, be gradual abolition in the end."  He went on to say that if the
Abolition movement tempered its radicalism it ran the danger of never
reaching its goal.  He favored constant pressure.

On How to Get From Here to There:

Since we haven't had this particular argument for a while, the newcomers
might appreciate my perspective.  Old timers will have seen it all 
before.

1)  We already have an anarchist society.  We call it Internet.  No one
knows or can know its extent.  It is filled with thousands of zones of
PPL.  It is outside of the control of any authority.  Its fundamental
programming resists censorship or restriction.  It grows by 20% every
three months.

2)  As more of our work and play is transferred to the nets, we will be
spending more and more of our time in an anarchist society in which 
contract is the only law.  

3)  Without anyone becoming a self-identified libertarian, everyone on
the nets will be living in a free society and developing the habits 
thereof.

4)  We are primates.  We tend to reflect the mores (memes) of the 
societies in which we live.  Living in a free society for part of the
time, we will begin to resent the restraints of the *physical* society
in which we spend the rest of our time.  In networking, we will have
the tools to allow us to overcome the restraints of the physical society.

5)  Government rests on acquiescence.  If the KOMMUNISTICHISKAYA PARTIYA
SOVYATSKOGO SOYUZA can fall, how much stronger can the Democratic Party
of the United States be.

6)  The networking model/meme/more is at work dedicatedly delayering
institutions.  The nation state is not exempt from this process.  Down-
sizing will occur there too.  To a network, I am the equal of Bill 
Clinton.  In fact, I have many more network addresses than Bill does.  On
the nets, I am in some sense his superior.

7)  As the market for nonphysical services grows and accounts for a larger
and larger portion of Gross World Product, the governments will shrink in
relative size.  Size is power.

8)  There is an over emphasis on government's ability to shoot people.  If
any nation's existence as a nation depends solely on its ability to shoot
people, it has already lost.  The nation state today will not be facing
a conspiracy or organized opposition of any kind.  It will be facing 
billions of people from all around the earth building alternative 
institutions and ways of living outside of its control.  The nation state
was invented about 1600 AD.  It is not written in stone.  The Church used
to be a major power center in Europe.  It no longer is.  That revolution
occurred with little bloodshed.

9)  The German Democratic Republic fell with a single question (after it
was weakened).  In November 1989 at an East German government press 
conference about liberalization of rules governing border crossings 
between the two Germanies, a British reporter asked One Last Question-
"What about crossing points in Berlin?"  The government spokesman tried
to say "well yes, but...".  Within hours the Wall was no more.

10)  Internationally, all nations are competing market actors (absent 
war).  There is no overall authority.  Legally, networks and the new
markets are international and governed by no overall authority.  If the
$1 Trillion daily volume of the international markets for foreign exchange
and "derivatives" equals the total foreign exchange reserves of all the
world's governments (and it does), the forex markets are hard to control.
What happens when the daily volume reaches $100 Trillion?

Just a few idle comments...

